WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.490 --> 00:00:27.140
This number… is illegal.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.140
Well kinda.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.419
I switched out a few digits to protect myself,
since, you know, it’s illegal, but just

00:00:32.419 --> 00:00:36.440
the possession of this number is enough to
get you arrested in the US.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.860
To understand why, we need to learn some cryptology.

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:44.010
Prime numbers are numbers that can only be
divided by themselves and one.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:49.050
For example, nothing multiplies together to
get seven other than seven and one, nothing

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:54.530
multiplies together to get 29 other than 29
and one, this will be the same for all primes.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:58.120
It’s not that hard to check if a smaller
number is prime.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.100
For seven you just check if seven divided
by one is a whole number, seven divided by

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:08.189
two, by three, by four, by five, by six, nothing
other than seven can divide seven into a whole

00:01:08.189 --> 00:01:09.189
number.

00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:12.610
As you get to bigger and bigger numbers you
have to check more and more times to determine

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:13.610
if a number is prime.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:14.610
For 43,721, you’d have to perform 21,861
calculations to check if its prime.

00:01:14.610 --> 00:01:15.610
As it turns out, it is.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.730
That means that its exceedingly difficult
to find new primes.

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:25.220
The largest prime that we know of right now
is 274,207,281-1.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:29.060
There is an infinite number of primes since
there is an infinite number of numbers, but

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.990
it just takes an enormous amount of computing
power to find these primes.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:34.970
So what’s the point of finding primes?

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.729
Well they’re actually quite useful for encryption.

00:01:37.729 --> 00:01:42.710
For the sake of simplicity, let’s take two
small primes, 11 and 13.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:46.789
If we were actually encrypting data, we would
be using primes with thousands of digits,

00:01:46.789 --> 00:01:50.259
but that would probably make this explanation
a little complicated.

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:58.639
Now multiply 11 and 13 together in your head.

00:01:58.639 --> 00:02:03.000
You can probably figure out pretty easily
that together they make 143.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.719
Now how long would it take you to work backwards
and figure out what multiplies together to

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:08.630
get 143?

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:09.760
Probably a pretty long time.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.530
It’s the same with computers.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:15.680
It’s very easy for them to multiply two
numbers together and find out what the product

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:20.820
is, but its very hard for them to take the
product and find out what the factors are.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.570
When you’re logging on to, say, your bank
account online, your computer will send the

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:29.210
number 143, which is known as the public key,
to the bank’s server.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:35.710
The bank will then check if 11 and 13, the
private keys, multiply to 143 and if so, it

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:36.940
will let you in.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:41.400
Everyone has the public key, but only the
bank has the private keys.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.440
If you wanted the find the private keys, you
would need a computer to factor the public

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.920
key, which would take thousands of years because,
once again, in the real world, the keys aren’t

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.570
11 and 13, they’re prime numbers with thousands
upon thousands of digits.

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.660
With the amount of money it would take to
set up a computer system that could actually

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:01.450
factor the primes, it’s just not worth it.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:05.310
The bigger the prime number, the longer it
would take to factor, and the more secure

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:06.310
it is.

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:11.060
That’s why organizations like the Electronic
Frontier Foundation are willing to pay up

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.770
to $250,000 for new prime numbers.

00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:18.269
Alright, that’s all the math we’re going
to do today, I promise.

00:03:18.269 --> 00:03:19.510
Back to our illegal number.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:24.900
In 1998, The Digital Millennium Copyright
Act was passed in the US, making it illegal

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:29.820
to circumvent copyright measures and to produce
or distribute tools that can be used to get

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.930
around copyright protection systems.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:37.790
Our illegal number, 8565078 and so on and
so forth, is a prime number that is the source

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:41.730
code used to decrypt the copyright protection
of DVD’s.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:46.230
The copyright protection was used to stop
people from downloading or duplicating movies

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:50.630
on their computers and, with this number,
the protection was rendered useless.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:54.920
Programs were created that allowed individuals
to make bootleg copies of movies all because

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.510
of this number.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:01.410
All the while, individuals were breaking the
law not only by copying DVD's, but also just

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:05.750
by being in possession of this number, an
easily findable prime.

